\title{tsorder: Order Selection for Time Series Models}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Order selection for time series models: classical information criteria (Akaike's information criterion (AIC) and its small-sample correction (AICc), the Bayesian information criterion (BIC), the Hannan-Quinn criterion (HQ), Akaike's final prediction error (FPE), Parzen's criterion autoregressive transfer function (CAT) and Rissanen's minimum description length (MDL), over Yule-Walker, Burg or conditional least-squares fits) side by side with the threshold ("clip") estimators of Fine and Hwang (1979) \doi{10.1109/TAC.1979.1102041} built on studentized statistics (the partial autocorrelation function (PACF), the autocorrelation function (ACF) with Bartlett's standard errors, the inverse PACF, the Jirak (2012) \doi{10.1214/11-AOS963} band, a robust PACF), plus McMillan-degree estimators (Bauer's (2001) \doi{10.1016/S0005-1098(01)00118-2} singular value criterion (SVC) on past-future canonical correlations, Hankel singular-value thresholding, a sequential rank test), autoregressive moving-average (ARMA) identification (extended sample autocorrelation function, Hannan-Rissanen), and the order of integration by sequential unit-root testing. All statistics run in a dependency-free Rust core.
}
